<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Sandomierz</h1><p class="st-meta">1138 CE – 1320 CE (approx.) · capital Sandomierz</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Piast duchy of eastern Lesser Poland around the Vistula town of Sandomierz, usually joined to Krakow in the age of division.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Boleslaw III</a>'s testament of 1138 assigned Sandomierz and its land to his young son Henry, who took control in 1146. Henry went on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> in 1154 and was killed fighting the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Prussians</a> in 1166, after which the duchy passed to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-ii-the-just">Casimir II the Just</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sandomierz was a prosperous trading town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, on the route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> and Rus'. For most of the period it was ruled by the same dukes as Krakow, so the two duchies formed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> sacked the town in 1241 and again in 1260, killing many of its inhabitants and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friars</a> of Saint James.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its rulers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-white">Leszek the White</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-v-the-chaste">Boleslaw V the Chaste</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-black">Leszek the Black</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek">Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high</a> gained Sandomierz in the early 14th century, and in 1320 it became part of the reunited <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385">Kingdom of Poland</a> (1025-1385).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-sandomierz.jpg" alt="Duchy of Sandomierz" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: Zuber &amp;amp; MariuszR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polska_za_W_Lokietka_(1275-1300).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1138 CE – 1320 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sandomierz</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henry of Sandomierz, Leszek the White, Boleslaw V the Chaste</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sandomierz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1229&amp;focus=duchy-of-sandomierz" aria-label="Duchy of Sandomierz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5176 418 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Sandomierz lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5496 674L5457 692"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5496 674h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5496 674h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sandomierz. <a href="/atlas?year=1229&amp;focus=duchy-of-sandomierz">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
